## Goal
A sample PCA from the gene counts, with one plot coloured by treated versus untreated: do the
six samples (A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) separate by condition along the leading principal
components?

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` (native) | `01_prepare_samplesheets` | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` environment
(pandas 3.0.5, pydeseq2 0.5.4, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1), submitted as one batch
job. Inputs are read only; nothing is written outside `results/`.

1. Resolve both inputs at run time with
   `python3 _system/resolve_artifact.py --project projects/run-1050456f --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design`;
   stop with an error if either fails to resolve.
2. Read the count matrix (tab-separated; `gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one column per sample).
   Index by `gene_id`, drop `gene_name`, transpose to samples x genes. Round the length-scaled
   counts to integers (required by DESeq2's count model; values are not otherwise altered).
3. Read the design table; align its rows to the count matrix's sample columns by `sample_id`.
   Stop with an error if the two sample sets differ.
4. Drop genes with zero counts in all samples.
5. Variance-stabilise with pydeseq2: `DeseqDataSet(counts, metadata, design="~condition")`,
   then `vst(use_design=False)` — a blind VST, so the treatment labels do not influence the
   transform the PCA sees.
6. Select the 500 most variable genes by variance of the VST values (or all genes, if fewer
   than 500 remain after step 4 — the matrix currently has 200), and centre each gene
   (no scaling), matching DESeq2's `plotPCA` defaults.
7. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(n_samples, n_genes))` (six samples →
   up to 6 components). Record per-sample scores and each component's explained-variance ratio.
8. Plot PC1 vs PC2 with matplotlib: one point per sample, coloured by `condition`
   (treated vs untreated, with a legend), each point labelled with its `sample_id`, axes
   labelled with the percentage of variance explained. Save as PNG at 300 dpi.
9. Write the scores table and the variance table (step 7) as TSVs.

## Outputs
Types come from the closed vocabulary in _references/artifact_types.md.

| File | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| results/pca_pc1_pc2_by_condition.png | figure | PC1 vs PC2 scatter, one point per sample, coloured treated vs untreated, axes showing % variance explained |
| results/pca_scores.tsv | table | One row per sample: `sample_id`, `condition`, and its score on every principal component |
| results/pca_variance_explained.tsv | table | One row per principal component: component, explained-variance ratio, cumulative ratio |

## Execution
Runs: batch
One Slurm job via the executor door (`_system/executorlib.py submit`), environment `gars-bio`,
with the resources the executor takes from `_config/` (it exposes no per-job override). Expected wall time under 5 minutes (6 samples x 200 genes); total output
under 1 MB.
